The '''Balboan Defence Forces''' (BDF; {{lang|ca|Forces Armades de Balboa}}, FAB) are the military forces of the [[Balboa|Republic of Balboa]]. The BDF consists of two branches: the [[Balboan Army|Army]] and the [[Balboan Naval and Air Service|Naval and Air Service]] (which includes the [[Balboan Coast Guard|Coast Guard]] and the [[Balboan Air Wing|Air Wing]]). The force comprises approximately 30,000 active personnel in an all-volunteer professional force. The [[President of Balboa]] serves as commander-in-chief, with the civilian [[Minister of Defence (Balboa)|Minister of Defence]] exercising day-to-day administrative oversight through the [[Ministry of Defence (Balboa)|Ministry of Defence]].

The BDF was established in 1974 following the [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973), which lifted the demilitarisation provisions that had been imposed on Balboa by the [[Treaty of Sant Llàtzer]] (1899) and restricted the country to a constabulary for internal security. During the [[Great Adonian War]], a secret [[Balboa Revolutionary Army]] funded by Volisania served as the primary Balboan combatant against the Zong military; both the Revolutionary Army and the constabulary were destroyed during the war. The BDF was built from scratch as a professional national defence force, distinct from both predecessor organisations. In its early years, the BDF also served as the principal security force outside the major cities, a role that persisted until the security reforms of the 1990s and the creation of the [[National Police force of Balboa]].

<!-- IMG: BDF naval exercise in the Balboa Canal approaches — a grey patrol boat cutting through turquoise tropical waters near a massive canal lock entrance, naval personnel in light blue uniforms on deck, lush green coastline in background, late afternoon golden light, wide shot -->
[[File:Balboa Canal defence exercise.png|thumb|right|300px|A BDF naval exercise in the Balboa Canal approaches. The defence of the canal is the BDF's primary mission.]]

The BDF's primary mission is the defence of the [[Balboa Canal]] and the country's territorial integrity. It also provides support to civilian law enforcement and to the [[International Cooperative Police Force]] (ICPF) in the event of a security emergency, and contributes small deployments to [[Adonian Community]] peacekeeping operations. The BDF is subject to the [[Twelve Guarantees]], including the provisions on non-discrimination and ethnic representation in recruitment and promotion.

== History ==

=== Demilitarisation and the constabulary (1899–1930s) ===

{{main|Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|Treaty of Sant Llàtzer}}

<!-- IMG: Balboan constabulary rump state era — a small formation of constabulary soldiers in outdated khaki uniforms standing at attention in a dusty provincial town square, white colonial-era buildings behind them, early 20th century photograph sepia tone, few in number, lightly armed with rifles, humid tropical atmosphere -->
[[File:Balboan constabulary rump state era.png|thumb|right|300px|The Balboan constabulary during the rump state period, c. 1930. The constabulary was the only military force permitted under the Treaty of Sant Llàtzer.]]

The [[Treaty of Sant Llàtzer]] (1899), which ended the [[Second War of the Isthmus]], imposed strict demilitarisation on the Republic of Balboa. The republic's armed forces were disbanded and replaced by a constabulary restricted to internal security duties. The constabulary was the only military force permitted under the treaty, and its size and equipment were limited by the terms of the settlement.

During the rump state period, the constabulary was expanded and repurposed by the planter oligarchy as an instrument of internal control, used to suppress labour organising on the plantations and political activity among the Black population. In the 1920s, [[Volisania]] began providing military assistance to Balboa in violation of the demilitarisation provisions, re-equipping the constabulary with modern weapons.

=== The Balboa Revolutionary Army (1930s–1947) ===

{{main|Balboa Revolutionary Army|Balboa Campaign|Great Adonian War}}

<!-- IMG: Balboa Revolutionary Army recruits training — a group of young white Balboan men in civilian clothes being drilled by a Volisanian military advisor in a hidden jungle clearing, 1930s era, wooden crates of rifles visible, dense tropical foliage, grainy historical photograph style, covert atmosphere -->
[[File:Balboa Revolutionary Army 1930s.png|thumb|right|300px|Balboa Revolutionary Army recruits during training, c. 1934. The army was organised in secret with Volisanian funding and arms in preparation for the invasion of Zong Balboa.]]

In the 1930s, Volisania secretly funded and organised the [[Balboa Revolutionary Army]] ({{lang|ca|Exèrcit Revolucionari de Balboa}}), a parallel military force distinct from the constabulary, to support the planned invasion of [[Zong Balboa]] and the recovery of the [[Balboa Canal]]. The Revolutionary Army was recruited, trained, and equipped covertly with Volisanian arms and funding, and its existence was concealed from the [[Adonian Community]] and from the treaty powers. The constabulary continued to serve as the official internal security force, but the Revolutionary Army was the principal military instrument intended for the offensive against Zong.

At the opening of the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947), the [[Balboa Campaign]] was launched as a joint Volisanian–Balboan offensive to retake Sant Cristòfor and the canal zone. The Revolutionary Army was the primary Balboan combatant against the Zong military during the campaign, providing ground forces for the assault on the canal zone while the constabulary was relegated to a supporting role. The Revolutionary Army sustained the bulk of the Balboan ground fighting throughout the early years of the war, but was unable to penetrate the walls of Sant Cristòfor, which held out for the duration of the conflict.

In 1942, Zong and Sinchew launched a northern campaign from the south, advancing up the isthmus and catching the Volisanian and Balboan forces off guard. The Revolutionary Army, which had been oriented toward the canal zone siege, was unable to mount an effective defence of the interior. Sant Bart was captured in 1943, and the remaining provincial centres fell in the following months. The Revolutionary Army disintegrated as a coherent force during the [[Zong occupation of Balboa (1943–1947)|Zong occupation of the interior]], and the constabulary, which had been the instrument of internal control, was likewise destroyed.

After the war, neither the constabulary nor the Revolutionary Army was reconstituted. The post-war republic lacked the resources and the institutional capacity to rebuild a military, and no Balboan armed forces existed for nearly three decades. Security and military functions in Balboa were provided by international forces in two phases.

From 1947 to 1952, the [[Unified Support Forces]] — a coalition comprising Zong, Louyang, Sinchew, and liberal Volisanian forces — provided military and security functions across the isthmus. The coalition was an ad hoc arrangement assembled in the immediate aftermath of the war, combining the occupying Zong forces and their allies with the liberal Volisanian forces that had overthrown the fascist government. The Unified Support Forces maintained order, supervised the disarmament of the former belligerents, and managed the initial reconstruction of the canal zone.

From 1952 to 1974, the [[Adonian Community]] assumed responsibility for military and security support through the [[AC Unified Forces]] and the [[International Cooperative Police Force]] (ICPF). The transition from the Unified Support Forces to the AC framework formalised the international presence under multilateral authority and placed it on a longer-term footing. The ICPF, established under the [[Treaty of Areza]] and the [[Twelve Guarantees]], operated throughout Balboa and provided the principal security presence during this period. A national police force that existed before reunification was disbanded during the implementation of the Twelve Guarantees because it had been associated with extrajudicial killings and state violence against minorities; many of its senior personnel transitioned into the military or later security structures.

=== Establishment (1973–1974) ===

{{main|Treaty of Areza|Constitution of Balboa}}

The [[Treaty of Areza]], signed on 1 July 1973, completed the reunification of the isthmus by returning the [[Balboa Canal]] zone to Balboan sovereignty and fully lifting the demilitarisation provisions of the Treaty of Sant Llàtzer. The treaty restored Balboa's right to maintain a standing military for the first time in seventy-four years and provided for the phased withdrawal of the [[AC Unified Forces]], whose security responsibilities were to be transferred to the new Balboan force.

<!-- IMG: BDF founding ceremony 1974 — a formal military ceremony in a highland city square, new recruits in crisp new olive-green uniforms standing in formation, a raised platform with government officials, Sant Bart highland architecture in background, overcast tropical sky, historic moment atmosphere, 1970s documentary photography style -->
[[File:BDF founding ceremony 1974.png|thumb|right|300px|The founding ceremony of the Balboan Defence Forces, Sant Bart, 1974. The first recruits were drawn predominantly from the Black and mixed Balboan communities that formed the political base of the Balboan People's Party.]]

The BDF was established in 1974 as a new professional force, built from scratch rather than from the remnants of the constabulary, the Revolutionary Army, or the pre-reunification police. The decision to build a new force reflected the political imperative of a clean break with the institutions of the rump state, which had been instruments of racial oppression, and the practical reality that both the constabulary and the Revolutionary Army had been destroyed during the war and no longer existed as coherent organisations. The first recruits were drawn from the Black and mixed Balboan communities that formed the political base of the [[Balboan People's Party]] (BPP), and the force was conceived from its inception as an instrument of the post-reunification state rather than of the planter class.

The early BDF faced significant challenges. The force had no institutional memory, no trained officer corps, and no equipment beyond what could be purchased from foreign suppliers. The [[Ministry of Defence (Balboa)|Ministry of Defence]] was established simultaneously, and the first minister was tasked with building an administrative and command structure from the ground up. The [[International Cooperative Police Force]] (ICPF), which had operated throughout Balboa since 1952 under the authority of the [[Treaty of Areza]] and the [[Twelve Guarantees]], provided substantial training and support to the BDF in its early years, furnishing instructors, doctrine, and institutional guidance that the new force lacked. The ICPF's two decades of experience in Balboa made it the principal institutional source of security expertise available to the BDF during its formation. The first years were marked by shortages of trained personnel, limited equipment, and the absence of a developed military doctrine.

In its initial form, the BDF functioned less as a conventional military than as a security force. The Army existed in two forms: a localised component that functioned largely as a jobs programme, providing employment to young men in rural areas while assisting with policing in the interior provinces, and a more professionalised Navy that was oriented primarily toward coast guard duties around the [[Balboa Canal]] and the country's coastal waters. In the early years, the BDF was employed principally in policing and internal security rather than in military action, reflecting both the absence of external threats and the country's need for a security presence in rural areas where no other state authority existed.

=== Early deployments and the Guarantees (1974–1976) ===

<!-- IMG: Siege of Murado Ranch 1974 — BDF soldiers in new olive-green fatigues crouched behind a sandbag perimeter at the edge of a plantation fence line, a large ranch house visible in the distance through banana trees, tense standoff atmosphere, soldiers holding rifles but not firing, tropical afternoon light, 1970s photojournalism style -->
[[File:Siege of Murado Ranch 1974.png|thumb|right|300px|BDF soldiers at the perimeter of the Murado Ranch during the siege of March 1974, the force's first operational deployment.]]

The BDF's first operational deployment came within months of its establishment. In March 1974, the [[Balboan Land and Reparations Authority]] issued an expropriation order for the [[Murado Ranch]], a large plantation in [[Tramuntana Province]] owned by one of the oldest planter dynasties in the province. The Murado family barricaded themselves with armed militia supporters, and the [[Miquel Torrent i Alvarado|Alvarado administration]] deployed the newly formed BDF to enforce the expropriation order. The [[Siege of Murado Ranch|siege]] lasted 18 days and ended without bloodshed, but it established the precedent that the federal government would use military force to enforce the [[Twelve Guarantees]] land redistribution programme.

The siege was followed by an escalation of white nationalist violence. The [[Primera Ordre de Balboa]] (POB), a white nationalist terrorist group, carried out the [[Sant Bart University bombing]] in February 1975, killing 42 people. The Alvarado administration responded with the [[La Guàrdia Campaign]] (1975–1976), a series of military and police incursions into the highlands of [[Jala Province|Jala]] to root out POB training camps and safe havens. The campaign was the largest domestic military operation in Balboan history and was the BDF's first sustained combat operation. The campaign significantly degraded the POB's operational capacity but also produced controversy: the government granted special arms licenses to [[Biru people|Biru]] communities in the highlands, a programme that had unintended consequences including the 1977 [[Aeroístmus hijacking]].

=== The Hill Wars and the domestic policing role (1980s) ===

{{main|Hill Wars|Illegal drug trade in Balboa}}

<!-- IMG: La Guàrdia Campaign 1975 — BDF soldiers moving in single file through dense highland cloud forest on a narrow mountain trail, mist and low clouds obscuring the canopy, soldiers carrying rifles and backpacks, point man scanning the treeline, steep terrain, humid mountain atmosphere, 1970s military photojournalism -->
[[File:La Guàrdia Campaign 1975.png|thumb|right|300px|BDF soldiers during the La Guàrdia Campaign in the Jala highlands, 1975. The campaign was the largest domestic military operation in Balboan history.]]

From the mid-1970s, illegal drug manufacturing took root in the highlands of [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] and [[Jala Province|Jala]], where the remote terrain and the absence of state authority created conditions for clandestine laboratories. The competition for territory and routes produced the [[Hill Wars]], a pattern of intermittent violence between rival drug factions in the Tramuntana highlands throughout the 1980s. The BDF was deployed to interdict drug production and to secure transportation routes through the highland provinces.

In 1983, the [[Roser Muntaner i Ferrer|Ferrer administration]] established the [[United Terrorism Taskforce]] (UTT), a joint military-police-intelligence body, to coordinate the response to the highland violence. The UTT created protected corridors — secured transportation routes through the highland provinces — to restore the state's ability to move people and goods without the risk of interdiction.

During this period, the BDF served as the default policing force outside the major cities. The arrangement had developed from the Army's localised component, which had been established in the early years as a rural presence and employment programme. Over time, these army units were drawn into the war on drug manufacturing in the northern regions, where they were used to destroy plantations and laboratories. The system became fragmented. Units in the interior had soldiers but little in the way of intelligence support, civilian negotiation capacity, or specialised internal-security training. The Navy, by contrast, remained more professionalised and better resourced, concentrated on canal and coast guard duties. Pay differed sharply by branch and role: the navy and central forces were better resourced than the patchwork army units that often functioned as local police. When local authorities encountered a crisis, the default response was often to dispatch troops and force compliance rather than to investigate and de-escalate.

From the 1960s through the 1980s, the BPP pushed to modernise the military and distance it from its corrupt and racist past. The 1990s became the main visible reform decade, when those long-running efforts became more concrete. During this period, the Naval and Air Service's Air Wing was equipped with helicopters and scouting aircraft donated from the surplus GAW materiel at the [[Areza joint military base|Areza military base]]. The Air Wing was conceived not as an independent combat arm but as a logistical and reconnaissance auxiliary, providing airlift, medical evacuation, and aerial observation in support of Army and naval operations.

=== Security reform and the separation of policing (1990s–2000s) ===

{{main|National Police force of Balboa|Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff}}

<!-- IMG: Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff 1999 — a military convoy of trucks and an armoured vehicle stopped on a rural dirt road in front of a fortified compound with high walls, BDF soldiers in confusion around the vehicles, smoke rising from the compound entrance, tense and chaotic atmosphere, late 1990s news photography style, overcast tropical light -->
[[File:Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff 1999.png|thumb|right|300px|The military convoy at the Hope and Survival Happiness Group compound, Estret Province, 1999. The botched raid became a catalyst for the creation of the National Police force of Balboa.]]

The weaknesses of the military-as-police model were exposed most dramatically in 1999, when the [[Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff]] demonstrated the BDF's lack of intelligence, negotiation, and civil-security capacity. The standoff began when a local mayor requested military support for a raid on a fortified compound in the [[Estret Province|Estret]] hills. The military platoon arrived with a tank and attempted to force entry, resulting in three deaths in the opening minutes before the operation was escalated to the president and the [[International Cooperative Police Force]] was called in to negotiate a surrender.

The standoff became one of the most cited examples of the weaknesses in Balboa's internal security system. It fed into the campaign for the creation of the [[National Police force of Balboa]] and a broader separation between domestic policing and military operations. The reforms that followed refocused the BDF on national defence rather than domestic policing, reduced troop numbers because of budget constraints, and established a civilian police institution with investigators, negotiators, and a civilian command structure separate from the military.

The [[ICPF]]'s mandate in Balboa proper was terminated in the 1990s as part of a phased reduction of AC oversight following Balboa's demonstrated compliance with the Guarantees. The ICPF's mandate in the [[International City of Areza|Areza]] remains perpetual.

=== Military modernisation (2001) ===

<!-- IMG: 2001 military modernisation — a BDF motor pool with new vehicles being unloaded from transport trucks at a base, soldiers inspecting new Baliscan-made trucks and Okaiken-built communications equipment, old GAW-era vehicles visible in the background for contrast, early 2000s military photography, daylight -->
In 2001, the BDF undertook a comprehensive modernisation programme, partially funded by a loan from the [[Adonian Community]], to replace its ageing inventory of surplus [[Great Adonian War]] equipment that had formed the backbone of the force since its establishment. The modernisation covered small arms, vehicles, naval vessels, and aircraft, with the new equipment sourced primarily from manufacturers in [[Balisca]], [[Okaiken]], and [[Volisania]]. The programme represented the first systematic replacement of the donated GAW materiel and brought the BDF's equipment closer to the standards of the AC coalition forces stationed in Balboa.

== Structure ==

The BDF consists of two branches. The Army and the Naval and Air Service were both established at the BDF's founding in 1974. The Air Wing operates as a component of the Naval and Air Service, reflecting the close operational relationship between the service's maritime patrol and coast guard duties and its aerial reconnaissance and search and rescue capabilities.

