=== Command structure ===

The [[President of Balboa]] serves as commander-in-chief of the BDF. The civilian [[Minister of Defence (Balboa)|Minister of Defence]], appointed by the president, exercises day-to-day administrative oversight through the [[Ministry of Defence (Balboa)|Ministry of Defence]]. The ministry is responsible for defence policy, budget management, procurement, and civilian oversight of the armed forces. The Minister of Defence reports to the president.

The [[Joint Military Council (Balboa)|Joint Military Council]] ({{lang|ca|Consell Militar Conjunt}}) is the principal military advisory and coordination body of the BDF, comprising the branch commanders of the Army and the Naval and Air Service and chaired by the chief of the defence staff. The council coordinates operations across the two branches, develops military doctrine, and provides unified military advice to the Minister of Defence. Each branch commander is responsible for the operational command of their respective branch and reports to the chief of the defence staff, who in turn reports to the minister.

Under the [[Constitution of Balboa]], an act of war requires the approval of the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]]. Once the assembly has approved, the president has leeway to direct military operations as commander-in-chief. The National Assembly also has the authority to withdraw from war. The president cannot unilaterally end or withdraw from a war, but may act as the government's agent in negotiating and signing treaties, subject to assembly ratification.

=== Bases and installations ===

<!-- IMG: Haicang naval base — an aerial view of a naval base on a tropical coastline, grey patrol boats docked at concrete piers, a shipyard with a vessel in dry dock, administrative buildings with red-tiled roofs, palm trees along the waterfront, Aethiopian Ocean stretching to the horizon, bright midday tropical light -->
[[File:Haicang naval base.png|thumb|right|300px|The Haicang naval base on the Aethiopian coast of Estret Province, the principal base of the Balboan Navy and the site of the Drassanes de Haicang shipyard.]]

The BDF's headquarters is located in [[Sant Bart]], [[Jala Province]], which serves as the principal administrative and command centre. The Army operates five bases, one in each province, plus the headquarters in Sant Bart. The Naval and Air Service operates three primary naval bases — at [[Haicang]] on the Aethiopian coast of [[Estret Province]], at [[Badia Curta]], and at [[Campdària]] — and approximately 28 naval stations along the coast at various points, which serve as forward patrol posts, rescue stations, and vessel refuelling points. The Haicang base also serves as the principal ship repair and maintenance facility. Specific base and station locations and capacities are detailed in the respective provincial articles.

<!-- IMG: Areza joint military base — a modern multinational military installation with multiple national flags flying at the entrance, concrete barracks and hangars, military vehicles with AC insignia, a perimeter fence with guard towers, flat tropical landscape, professional military photography, clear sky -->
In addition to the BDF's own installations, Balboa hosts two major foreign military facilities under the [[Adonian Community]] framework. The [[Areza joint military base]], located in the [[International City of Areza]], is a coalition installation that houses AC member state forces and serves as the principal forward operating base for the AC's collective defence presence on the isthmus. The [[AC Haicang Navy Station]], co-located with the BDF naval base at Haicang, is leased to the Adonian Community for the docking and maintenance of foreign military vessels. The presence of these installations is a central element of Balboa's defence strategy, under which the BDF provides domestic security and canal defence while the AC coalition forces provide the principal deterrent against external aggression.

<!-- IMG: BDF civilian personnel — a military base administrative office interior, civilian employees in business-casual attire working at desks with computers and filing cabinets, a cafeteria counter visible through an open doorway in the background, a janitor pushing a cleaning cart down a corridor, natural daylight from large windows, documentary photography style -->
=== Civilian personnel ===

In addition to the approximately 30,000 active-duty military personnel, the BDF employs a civilian workforce managed by the [[Ministry of Defence (Balboa)|Ministry of Defence]]. Civilian employees work across the full range of support functions, including cafeteria and food service staff, janitorial and facilities maintenance workers, administrative clerks, legal advisors and paralegals, procurement and contract specialists, financial analysts, human resources personnel, and information technology support. The civilian workforce allows the BDF to maintain its bases and installations without diverting military personnel from operational duties, and the Ministry of Defence exercises direct administrative authority over civilian hiring, compensation, and management.

=== Rank structure ===

The BDF uses Volisanian-style rank terminology, consistent with the Volisanian language used in Balboan government and institutions. The army and air force share a similar rank structure, while the navy uses a separate naval rank system.

