== The Twelve Guarantees and the military ==

{{main|Twelve Guarantees}}

The BDF is subject to the [[Twelve Guarantees]], the entrenched constitutional provisions that guarantee full civil rights, anti-discrimination protections, and ethnic representation in Balboan institutions. The Guarantees apply to military recruitment, promotion, and conduct, and the BDF is required to maintain non-discriminatory practices and to ensure ethnic representation consistent with the Guarantees' provisions.

The application of the Guarantees to the military was a significant departure from the rump state period, when the constabulary had been an instrument of racial oppression. The BDF's recruitment base, drawn predominantly from the Black and mixed Balboan communities that formed the BPP's political base, was itself a reflection of the Guarantees' impact on the military's composition. The integration of the Guarantees into military practice was a gradual process, and the BPP's efforts to modernise the military and distance it from its racist past extended from the 1960s through the 1980s.

[[File:BDF budget infographic.png|thumb|right|450px|BDF budget 2026: approximately $2.4 billion, representing about 0.7 per cent of GDP, and a total workforce of roughly 33,275 across military and civilian employees.]]

== Budget ==

The BDF's budget is approximately $2.4 billion (2026), representing approximately 0.7 per cent of Balboa's gross domestic product. The budget is modest by international standards, reflecting Balboa's small size, the absence of significant external threats, and the country's reliance on the [[Adonian Community]] collective security framework for strategic deterrence. Balboa's defence strategy is based on hosting AC coalition forces rather than maintaining a large standing military of its own; the presence of foreign forces at the [[Areza joint military base]] and the [[AC Haicang Navy Station]] provides the principal deterrent against external aggression. The budget has been constrained since the 1990s reforms, when troop numbers were reduced as part of the refocusing of the military on national defence rather than domestic policing.

